          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 52-year-old woman with established seropositive rheumatoid arthritis develops </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 52-year-old woman with established seropositive rheumatoid arthritis develops hard, bony enlargements of her DIP joints over several months. Her MCP and PIP joints show typical RA changes, but her DIP joints were previously uninvolved. What is the most likely cause of the new DIP findings?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Progression of rheumatoid arthritis to involve the DIP joints</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>RA characteristically spares the DIP joints. Even in advanced disease, DIP involvement is not a feature of RA, so these findings need a different explanation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coexisting osteoarthritis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hard, bony DIP enlargements are Heberden's nodes — the hallmark of osteoarthritis. Since RA spares the DIP joints, new bony DIP changes in an RA patient indicate concurrent OA, which is common in this age group.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gouty tophi depositing at the DIP joints</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gouty tophi are firm to soft chalky deposits, not hard bony enlargements. Their appearance and texture differ from the bony Heberden's nodes described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Erosive disease extending from the adjacent PIP joints</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>RA erosions do not extend anatomically from one joint to the next. The DIP and PIP joints are separate articulations, and RA's pattern of DIP sparing holds regardless of PIP disease severity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key principle is that RA characteristically spares the DIP joints. Hard, bony DIP enlargements are Heberden's nodes of osteoarthritis. Both conditions can coexist in the same patient."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. RA spares the DIP joints, so hard bony DIP enlargements in an RA patient represent coexisting osteoarthritis (Heberden's nodes) — a common clinical overlap."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
