          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with rheumatoid arthritis asks why she needs methotrexate when ibuprof</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with rheumatoid arthritis asks why she needs methotrexate when ibuprofen already controls her pain. What is the most accurate explanation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Methotrexate provides stronger pain relief than ibuprofen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Methotrexate is not primarily an analgesic. Its therapeutic value lies in suppressing the immune-mediated process that destroys joints, not in direct pain control.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Methotrexate targets the immune process that destroys joints, while ibuprofen only controls symptoms</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DMARDs like methotrexate modify the disease course by suppressing autoimmune synovitis and pannus formation, preventing irreversible erosions. NSAIDs relieve pain and stiffness but cannot halt joint destruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Methotrexate has fewer adverse effects than long-term NSAID use</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Methotrexate carries significant risks including hepatotoxicity, bone marrow suppression, and teratogenicity. Its advantage over NSAIDs is disease modification, not a better safety profile.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Methotrexate cures rheumatoid arthritis permanently</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Methotrexate controls RA but does not cure it. It must be continued long-term under the treat-to-target strategy; stopping it risks disease flare and renewed joint damage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: NSAIDs control symptoms; DMARDs change the disease course. Methotrexate suppresses the autoimmune process driving pannus formation and joint erosion — something no NSAID can do."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The key distinction is that only DMARDs prevent joint destruction by targeting the underlying immune process. NSAIDs provide symptomatic relief but cannot stop erosive damage from progressing."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
