          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 32-year-old man presents with swollen DIP joints, a sausage-shaped left index </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 32-year-old man presents with swollen DIP joints, a sausage-shaped left index finger, and scaly plaques on his elbows. Rheumatoid factor is negative. Which diagnosis is most likely?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rheumatoid arthritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>RA spares the DIP joints, does not cause dactylitis (sausage digits), and is RF-positive in about 70% of cases. This presentation contradicts RA on all three counts.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ankylosing spondylitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>AS primarily involves the axial skeleton — sacroiliac joints and spine — not the DIP joints of the hands. While dactylitis can occur in spondyloarthropathies, the combination with DIP disease and psoriasis points to PsA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Psoriatic arthritis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DIP joint arthritis, dactylitis (sausage digit), psoriatic skin plaques, and a negative rheumatoid factor are the hallmark combination of psoriatic arthritis — its two pathognomonic features being DIP involvement and dactylitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteoarthritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>OA can involve DIP joints (Heberden's nodes), but dactylitis and psoriatic plaques in a 32-year-old with soft-tissue swelling are not features of degenerative disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the two anchor features for psoriatic arthritis: DIP joint involvement and dactylitis (sausage digit). These never occur in RA, and combined with psoriatic skin changes and negative RF, they clinch the diagnosis of PsA."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. DIP arthritis and dactylitis are essentially pathognomonic for psoriatic arthritis — they never occur in RA. Combined with psoriatic skin lesions and a negative RF, the diagnosis is clear."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
